## The Monday-morning ritual

Every quarterly review starts the same way:

::: {.incremental}
- Re-run the analysis in a notebook
- Copy the charts into slides, one by one
- Re-do the formatting the template broke
- Notice a number changed, and start over
- Present numbers that were already a week old
:::

. . .

None of that is *analysis*. It is **assembly**, and you pay for it every week.

## What assembly costs

Not pasted in: computed when the slide renders, from the same numbers as the report.

```{python}
#| echo: false
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

analysts = np.arange(2, 26, 2)
hours_per_week, weeks, loaded_rate = 7, 46, 65          # assembly hours, working weeks, $/hr
cost_k = analysts * hours_per_week * weeks * loaded_rate / 1000

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8.2, 2.9), tight_layout=True)
ax.bar(analysts, cost_k, width=1.5, color="#e0566b", alpha=0.9)
ax.set_xlabel("analysts on the team")
ax.set_ylabel("$k / year spent assembling decks")
ax.set_title("7 hours a week, per analyst, that never touched the analysis")
for s in ("top", "right"):
    ax.spines[s].set_visible(False)
plt.show()
```

A twelve-person team burns roughly a quarter of a million dollars a year **moving
numbers between tools**.

## What if the slides *were* the analysis? {background-image="assets/backdrop.svg"}

One `.tmd` file. The code that computes the number and the slide that shows it are
the same document, so the number is never stale and never re-typed.

## One source, every surface

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  Q["post.tmd<br/>(analysis + narrative)"] --> D["live deck<br/>(this talk)"]
  Q --> R["the written report"]
  Q --> W["a page on the portal"]
```

Write it once. Present it, send it, and publish it, from the same file, with the
same numbers.

## The model is one line

The lifetime value of a seat is its revenue divided by how fast you lose it:

$$
\text{LTV} = \frac{\text{ARR}}{\text{churn}}
$$

At 5% monthly churn a seat is worth ~20× its ARR; at 8%, only ~12×. **The
assumption *is* the business case**, which is exactly why you want to answer it in
the room.

## ...and the model is the code

The same formula, walked line by line as you talk:

```{.python code-line-numbers="1|2-3|4-5|all"}
def five_year_runrate(seats, arr, churn):
    months = range(60)
    retained = [seats * (1 - churn) ** m for m in months]
    revenue = [r * arr for r in retained]      # annual run-rate each month
    return revenue[-1] / 1000                   # $k at month 60
```

## Refine it without leaving the room

Someone asks about expansion revenue. You edit the model **live**, and the matched
lines glide into place:

::: {.magic-move}
```python
def five_year_runrate(seats, arr, churn):
    retained = seats * (1 - churn) ** 60
    return retained * arr / 1000
```

```python
def five_year_runrate(seats, arr, churn, expansion=0.02):
    net = (1 - churn) + expansion          # net revenue retention
    retained = seats * net ** 60
    return retained * arr / 1000
```
:::

## The number, computed live

Churn of 5% versus 8% is not a rounding error. The shaded gap is the cost of
*guessing*:

```{python}
#| echo: false
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

m = np.arange(0, 61)
seats, arr = 100, 2400
runrate = lambda c: seats * (1 - c) ** m * arr / 1000   # $k annual run-rate

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8.2, 3.1), tight_layout=True)
ax.plot(m, runrate(0.05), lw=2.6, color="#27a567", label="churn 5%")
ax.plot(m, runrate(0.08), lw=2.6, color="#e0566b", label="churn 8%")
ax.fill_between(m, runrate(0.08), runrate(0.05), color="#e0566b", alpha=0.12)
ax.set_xlabel("month")
ax.set_ylabel("annual run-rate ($k)")
ax.legend(frameon=False, loc="upper right")
for s in ("top", "right"):
    ax.spines[s].set_visible(False)
plt.show()
```

## So don't guess. Let them turn the dial. {background-gradient="linear-gradient(135deg,#1b2550,#3a2a6a)"}

The slowest two words in any meeting are *"I'll follow up."*

## Hand the client the slider

Drag the assumption. The answer updates the instant you let go, no rebuild, no
follow-up email:

```{=html}
<style>
.tali-deck section label { font: 500 .45em/1.4 system-ui; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .7rem; margin: .3em 0; }
.tali-deck section input[type=range] { width: 22rem; }
</style>
```

```{js}
//| viewof: churn
const label = document.createElement("label");
const range = document.createElement("input");
range.type = "range";
range.min = "0.02"; range.max = "0.15"; range.step = "0.005"; range.value = "0.05";
label.append("churn / month", range);
return label;
```

```{js}
//| input: churn
const churn = qmd.value("churn");
const retained = Math.pow(1 - churn, 60);
const arr = Math.round(2400 * retained);
const pct = (100 * retained).toFixed(0);
const div = document.createElement("div");
div.style.cssText = "font:600 1.15em system-ui;color:var(--qmd-deck-fg,#e8eaf0)";
div.innerHTML = `
    Retained after 5 years: <span style="color:#5b8cff">${pct}%</span> of the cohort
    &nbsp;·&nbsp; <span style="color:#27a567">$${arr.toLocaleString()}</span> run-rate / 100 seats
    <div style="height:16px;margin-top:.5em;border-radius:8px;background:#2a3350;overflow:hidden">
      <div style="height:100%;width:${pct}%;background:#5b8cff;transition:width .2s"></div>
    </div>`;
return div;
```

## Two plans, side by side

::: {layout-ncol=2}
### Status quo
- Decks assembled by hand
- Numbers a week old
- "I'll get back to you"
- ~$250k/yr in assembly

### Live decks
- Analysis *is* the deck
- Numbers current to the second
- Answered in the meeting
- That time goes back to analysis
:::

## What it's worth {background-color="#0f172a"}

::: {.callout-tip}
## The two line items
1. **Reclaimed time** — ~7 analyst-hours/week, redirected from assembly to insight.
2. **Decision latency** — every *"I'll follow up"* that becomes *"here's your answer"* pulls a decision (and its revenue) forward.
:::

| Lever | Before | After |
|---|---:|---:|
| Deck assembly (12 analysts) | ~$250k / yr | redirected to analysis |
| Time-to-answer in a review | days | seconds |
| Risk of a stale number | every deck | structurally impossible |

## Present like it's a product, not a chore

::: {.incremental}
- <kbd>S</kbd> — **speaker view**: current + next slide, your notes, a timer
- <kbd>Ctrl/Cmd-P</kbd> — a clean **one-slide-per-page PDF** to send afterwards
- <kbd>O</kbd> — **overview**: every slide at a glance, jump anywhere
- <kbd>D</kbd> — **draw** on the slide to make a point
- <kbd>M</kbd> — the **menu**: jump, toggle, switch theme
- <kbd>F</kbd> — **fullscreen**; on a phone it flattens to a scrollable read
:::

::: {.notes}
Speaker notes live in a `::: {.notes}` block and only appear in speaker view (press
S). Reminder: pause on the live chart, then drag the churn slider yourself before
handing it over.
:::

